---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import WaterfallGallery from '../components/WaterfallGallery.astro';
import VariedLanes from '../components/VariedLanes.astro';
import SpanningCards from '../components/SpanningCards.astro';
import PlacedItems from '../components/PlacedItems.astro';
import BrickLayout from '../components/BrickLayout.astro';
import FlowTolerance from '../components/FlowTolerance.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="CSS Grid Lanes Demo">
  <section>
    <h2 class="example__title">1. Basic waterfall gallery</h2>
    <p class="example__desc">
      The classic masonry layout in three lines of CSS. Each card flows into
      whichever lane gets it closest to the top — like cars in bumper-to-bumper
      traffic, always changing lanes to stay furthest ahead.
    </p>
    <p><code class="example__code">display: grid-lanes; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem;</code></p>
    <WaterfallGallery />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="example__title">2. Varying lane sizes</h2>
    <p class="example__desc">
      Because lanes are defined with the full power of <code>grid-template-columns</code>,
      you can mix narrow and wide lanes — and keep the first and last lanes narrow
      no matter how many lanes the viewport creates.
    </p>
    <p><code class="example__code">grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(8rem, 1fr) minmax(16rem, 2fr)) minmax(8rem, 1fr);</code></p>
    <VariedLanes />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="example__title">3. Spanning items</h2>
    <p class="example__desc">
      Items can span multiple lanes with <code>grid-column: span N</code>, enabling
      asymmetric editorial designs — a lead story across four lanes, teasers across two.
    </p>
    <p><code class="example__code">article {'{'} grid-column: span 2; {'}'} article:first-child {'{'} grid-column: span 4; {'}'}</code></p>
    <SpanningCards />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="example__title">4. Explicit placement</h2>
    <p class="example__desc">
      You can still place items explicitly. This header is pinned to the last two
      lanes — whatever their total number is.
    </p>
    <p><code class="example__code">header {'{'} grid-column: -3 / -1; {'}'}</code></p>
    <PlacedItems />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="example__title">5. Brick layout — lanes in the other direction</h2>
    <p class="example__desc">
      Define lanes with <code>grid-template-rows</code> instead, and items flow
      left-to-right in a "brick" layout rather than top-to-bottom.
    </p>
    <p><code class="example__code">display: grid-lanes; grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr 1fr;</code></p>
    <BrickLayout />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="example__title">6. Flow tolerance</h2>
    <p class="example__desc">
      <code>flow-tolerance</code> controls how picky the placement algorithm is.
      At <code>0</code>, items chase every tiny height advantage; at a high value,
      items keep their reading order and only change lanes for a real difference.
      The default is <code>1em</code>.
    </p>
    <FlowTolerance />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
